<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01436#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is projected onto the visual material of the frog, which is used as a metaphorical stand-in for the autistic kid described in the text. The frog's physical resilience and unharmed state are visually aligned with the fictional 'tard strength' concept from the text, creating a semiotic projection where the viewer imagines themselves or identifies with the frog's metaphorical role in the absurd scenario. This projection is implicit, not explicit (e.g., no 'you' or 'me' deictic), but is activated by the meme's structure which maps the frog onto the autistic kid's fictional invulnerability."@en .
_:n1aa1e0e4b6fe4fe2bea80f2d06da1e6db101 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:n1aa1e0e4b6fe4fe2bea80f2d06da1e6db102 .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#MotionBlurIntegrated> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01436#toxicity_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme trivializes a serious and traumatic event by reducing the suffering of autistic individuals to a joke, potentially causing emotional harm and reinforcing dangerous stereotypes."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#temporallyRelatedTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
